From: Uzair Aftab <git@uzaaft.me>
Date: Mon, 12 Oct 2026 11:41:25 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] fix textview wrapping of multi-byte text

When wrapping was on, TextView split each line every width bytes. A
multi-byte character, like a box-drawing line, got cut in half at the
wrap point and drawn as U+FFFD replacement characters. Wide characters
were miscounted too, so CJK text wrapped well before the edge. In k9s
this shows up in every wrapped view that has non-ASCII text, e.g. the
border lines of the error ("Ruroh?") screen.

The wrap now uses runewidth.Truncate, as the old BOZO comment
suggested. It walks grapheme clusters and measures them the same way
stringWidth does, so lines are cut at the screen width and combining
marks or emoji sequences stay intact. A cluster wider than the view
still falls through to the existing single-cluster extraction.
---
 textview.go | 11 ++++-------
 1 file changed, 4 insertions(+), 7 deletions(-)

diff --git a/textview.go b/textview.go
index 3cc840d..6fe0da5 100644
--- a/textview.go
+++ b/textview.go
@@ -10,6 +10,7 @@ import (
 
 	"github.com/derailed/tcell/v2"
 	colorful "github.com/lucasb-eyer/go-colorful"
+	"github.com/mattn/go-runewidth"
 	"github.com/rivo/uniseg"
 )
 
@@ -772,13 +773,9 @@ func (t *TextView) reindexBuffer(width int) {
 		if t.wrap && len(strippedStr) > 0 {
 			var extract string
 			for len(strippedStr) > 0 {
-				// !!BOZO!! This is a dog. Would this work instead??
-				// extract := runewidth.Truncate(strippedStr, width, "")
-				if len(strippedStr) > width {
-					extract = strippedStr[:width]
-				} else {
-					extract = strippedStr
-				}
+				// Cut at the screen width, not the byte length, so multi-byte
+				// and wide characters aren't split or miscounted.
+				extract = runewidth.Truncate(strippedStr, width, "")
 
 				if len(extract) == 0 {
 					// We'll extract at least one grapheme cluster.
